#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087443 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bea23649-e835-540a-b238-7b89ed330cd3" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Inverse of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "318fc39e-0efe-5b98-968c-ff4408706471" :question/math-academy-id "e-4502" :question/problem "Calculate $A^{-1}$, where\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "Given a $2 \\times 2$ matrix\n$\\begin{bmatrix}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nthe inverse of $A$ can be found using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA^{-1} &= (1)/(det(A))\\begin{bmatrix}d & -b \\\\ -c & a\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\det(A) = 1 \\cdot 1 - 0 \\cdot (-2) = 1\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, then, the inverse is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA^{-1} &= (1)/(det(A))\\begin{bmatrix}d & -b \\\\ -c & a\\end{bmatrix}. \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -0 \\\\ - (-2) & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can verify the result as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAA^{-1} &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= I_{2}✓ \\\\\nA^{-1}A &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= I_{2}✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087444 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c6056ee-15f3-5b5c-8a14-173ea1da1381" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Singular Matrices"}] :question/id #uuid "195d7dab-edf2-536b-a7b7-d1c16c7b1071" :question/math-academy-id "e-4503" :question/problem "Which of the matrices below are singular?\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "- For the matrix $A$, we have\n$\\det({A})=1 \\cdot 1 - 1 \\cdot 1 = 0$.\nBecause $\\det(A)=0$, we conclude that $A$ is not invertible. Since $A$ is a square matrix that is not invertible, we also say that $A$ is singular.\n- The matrix $B$ is a $3 \\times 2$ matrix. So, it is not invertible. Since the matrix is not square, we can't say that it's singular.\n- For the matrix $C$, we have\n$\\det({C})=3 \\cdot 3 - (-3) \\cdot 3 = 18$.\nBecause $\\det(C) \\neq 0$, we conclude that $C$ is invertible.\n\nTherefore, only $A$ is singular."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087445 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4e64e0c3-f8f5-5c38-ae75-1aed92e0ac01" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for a Variable in a Singular Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "ef06dd89-501b-5d2b-a56d-4554a049a91d" :question/math-academy-id "e-4504" :question/problem "If\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 - k & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1 - k\\end{bmatrix}$\nis a singular matrix, calculate the value(s) of $k$." :question/worked-solution "The matrix $A$ is singular if and only if $\\det(A)=0$. Calculating the determinant, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ndet(A) &= (1 - k)(1 - k) - 4 \\\\\n&= k^{2} - 2k - 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set the determinant equal to $0$ and solve for $k$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nk^{2} - 2k - 3 &= 0 \\\\\n(k - 3)(k + 1) &= 0 \\\\\nk &= 3,-1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the values of $k$ are $k=3$ and $k=-1$."}]}
